[h1]Arcane Power[/h1]

[i]There is something moving inside the containment field.
The instruments call it a reaction. The engineers call it a power source.
Neither explanation makes the emergency shutters feel thick enough.[/i]

You have learned how to wake it. You have learned how to draw power from it.
[b]Make sure you also learn how to let it go.[/b]

Arcane Power brings industrial arcane reactors, rare fuels, expendable containment and modular energy storage to Space Engineers. Build the infrastructure, feed the field, and keep a clear route to the stars when containment begins to fail.

[h2]The Reactor[/h2]

[list]
[*]A large-grid reactor with animated assembly, rotating containment rings, visible tile replacement, shutdown and core ejection
[*]Three fuel tiers with provisional maximum outputs of [b]10 GW, 100 GW and 1 TW[/b]
[*]Standard and advanced external containment controllers; two controllers are required, with their combination determining two, three or four deployed rings
[*]Load-dependent fuel consumption, a gradual output ramp and heat-sensitive operation
[*]Real grid-powered containment, startup precharge and a stored shutdown reserve
[*]Original models, materials, icons, sound cues and three welding/grinding stages for every public block
[/list]


[h2]Feed the Field[/h2]

Discover [b]Aetherite, Helionite and Empyrite[/b] in procedural asteroids. Refine the ores into dense concentrates, then assemble Aether, Helion and Empyrean fuel cartridges. Higher fuels require the previous fuel tier and rarer concentrate.

Containment uses a shell of [b]80 tiles[/b]. Standard, Reinforced and Advanced tiles provide progressively longer service life. Mixed-grade shells are supported, and the reactor replaces worn tiles in visible groups of four. Conveyors can supply fuel and replacement tiles automatically.

Ordinary shutdown retains the shell. [b]Ejection loses it.[/b] Neglected maintenance reduces output, wastes fuel and can turn a manageable heat problem into an irreversible critical reaction.

[h2]Give It a Way Out[/h2]

Install a straight, aligned stack of [b]one to fifteen vent ducts and a terminal outlet[/b] above the reactor. The route must be complete before startup. Keep the outlet clear and point it somewhere expendable.

[list]
[*]Coordinated shields, reactor hatches and outlet shutters
[*]Manual ejection and damage-triggered emergency ejection at 60% remaining reactor integrity, provided the controllers and vent route are intact
[*]Physical spent rings, tiles and an ejected core that carry momentum into the world
[*]Heat- and load-dependent wear from venting; critical reactions retain their fuse after ejection
[*]A broken hardware route pauses a committed launch while heat and the critical fuse continue
[*]Security mode shuts the reactor down when its vent route is lost; disabling it allows an already-running reactor to continue with a warning
[/list]

[b]Ejecting a critical core moves the danger. It does not cancel it.[/b]


[h2]Arcane Energy Core[/h2]

A [b]3 x 3 x 3 large-grid battery installation[/b] stores surplus power and supports startup, cooling and emergency operation.

[list]
[*]Base capacity: [b]1,000 MWh[/b], with a [b]10 GW[/b] transfer ceiling
[*]Two side capacity sockets: standard modules add [b]10,000 MWh[/b] each; advanced modules add [b]100,000 MWh[/b] each
[*]A rear transfer module raises the bank ceiling to [b]100 GW or 1 TW[/b]
[*]A front stabilizer enables matching or lower-tier expansion modules
[*]Native Auto, Recharge and Discharge modes, with 90% charging efficiency
[*]Configurable emergency reserve; an eligible reactor can release it and switch Recharge to Auto
[*]A luminous charge-responsive sphere, reactor-style gyroscopic rings and flow-color indicators
[/list]

All upgrade pillars occupy [b]1 x 3 x 1 full large-grid cells[/b], with curved backs fitted around the core. Modules must face the correct core socket, match its Up direction, be fully built and remain on the same physical grid. Charge belongs to each battery: detached capacity retains its energy, destruction loses it, and new/projected/copied storage starts empty. Vanilla batteries remain a valid startup and backup option.


[h2]Know What Is Happening[/h2]

Use the reactor terminal, the [b]Arcane Reactor[/b] LCD script, or the handheld reactor tablet to monitor operation, fuel, tile supplies, heat, containment power and warnings. Tablet telemetry requires an accessible antenna connection; it does not grant remote reactor control. Text HUD API is optional for the richer handheld display.

[h2]Getting Started[/h2]

[olist]
[*]Enable the mod in an experimental Space Engineers world.
[*]Find the blocks in the shared [b]Arcane Systems[/b] build-menu category.
[*]Fully weld the reactor, attach two suitable containment controllers and complete its duct/outlet stack.
[*]Provide external startup power, compatible fuel and containment tiles.
[*]Check the terminal interlocks, enable the reactor and allow precharge and assembly to finish.
[/olist]

[h2]Compatibility and Development[/h2]

Arcane Power works independently of Ship Core Framework and Arcane Cores. Animation is controlled by the mod itself; Animation Engine is not required.

This is an evolving release. Ratings, recipes and costs are provisional. Dedicated-server/late-join behavior, sustained rated-load balance and the complete survival progression still need broader testing. Blueprint illustrations are renders of the authored models, not in-game screenshots.

